#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""A stand-in for ``kiro-cli`` in the Handoff_Harness tests of ``scripts/tests/``.

The tests start this script where the harness would start ``kiro-cli``. The
``handoff_sandbox`` fixture of ``scripts/tests/conftest.py`` copies it, with its mode, to
``~/.local/bin/kiro-cli`` of the sandbox home. It accepts the argv that
``handoff.command.build_kiro_argv`` builds, appends what it received to a record file, and
behaves as the environment asks. It never calls the real ``kiro-cli``, opens no network
connection, and writes only to the record, to its standard output and standard error, and
to the git repositories that the behaviors below name.

It runs under whichever ``python3`` comes first on ``PATH`` (the venv's 3.12 under
``make test`` and in CI, a newer one when pytest is started by hand), so it uses the
standard library only and no syntax newer than Python 3.8.

Invocations. Anything else is a usage error (exit 2):

- ``kiro-cli --version`` prints ``kiro-cli 0.0.0-fake``, or ``kiro-cli <FAKE_KIRO_VERSION>``,
  and exits 0, or exits 1 with ``version-fail``.
- ``kiro-cli whoami`` prints a made-up account and exits 0, or exits 1 with ``whoami-fail``.
- ``kiro-cli chat --no-interactive [--trust-tools=<names> | --trust-all-tools | -a]
  [--agent <name> | --agent=<name>] [--] <prompt>`` runs the behaviors of
  ``FAKE_KIRO_MODE`` in order and then writes ``FAKE_KIRO_ANSWER``. ``--no-interactive``
  and exactly one prompt are required, ``--trust-tools`` is accepted in its ``=`` form only
  (the form the harness builds), and an unknown option is refused rather than ignored, so
  that an argv the harness should not build fails the test. The trust options are
  recorded, not enforced: a behavior runs whatever tools the argv trusts. The one
  exception is ``write-file``, which stands for a ``kiro-cli`` that enforces them.

Selftest trials. A selftest of the harness (task 5.2) starts ``chat`` once per trial, T0 to
T4, and each prompt holds the trial's token ``handoff-selftest-t<n>-<16 lowercase hex
digits>``, with no letter, digit or ``_`` right before or after it. When the prompt holds
one token of trial ``t<n>`` and ``FAKE_KIRO_MODE_T<n>`` is set, that variable replaces
``FAKE_KIRO_MODE`` for this run, so that one selftest gets different behaviors per trial.
A prompt with two different tokens is a usage error. The token also names what the
selftest behaviors below commit and write; without one they write ``fake-kiro-selftest``
in its place.

Environment. Every name starts with ``FAKE_KIRO_``, because ``handoff.command.child_env``
drops the variables whose names start with ``AWS_``, ``GIT_``, ``GH_`` or ``GITHUB_``. The
exception is ``HANDOFF_GUARD_LOG``, the harness's variable that the guard behaviors write
to as the guard wrapper of the selftest would (design note of task 5.2).

``FAKE_KIRO_MODE``
    Behaviors separated by commas, run in the order given. None by default.
    ``whoami-fail`` applies to ``whoami`` and ``version-fail`` to ``--version``; the others
    apply to ``chat``. An unknown name is a usage error.

    - ``whoami-fail``: ``whoami`` writes an error to standard error and exits 1.
    - ``version-fail``: ``--version`` writes an error to standard error and nothing to
      standard output, and exits 1, as a ``kiro-cli`` whose version cannot be read.
    - ``noise``: writes ``FAKE_KIRO_NOISE_BYTES`` bytes (1 MiB by default) to standard
      output and as many to standard error, in alternating chunks, so a reader that drains
      one stream before the other blocks once the other pipe is full. Both payloads hold
      bytes that are not UTF-8 and multibyte characters, so that a reader that decodes the
      output in pieces (of 4 KiB to 64 KiB at the default size) gets a different text from
      one that decodes it whole. :func:`noise_payload` returns the bytes.
    - ``ignore-sigterm``: from here on, the script and the children it starts ignore
      SIGTERM, so only SIGKILL stops them.
    - ``sleep``: starts a child process (``python -c`` with ``time.sleep``) in the script's
      process group, appends a ``spawned`` event with its PID once the child's code runs,
      sleeps ``FAKE_KIRO_SLEEP_SECONDS`` (30 by default), then kills and reaps the child.
      The child sleeps 30 seconds longer than the script, so a run that is stopped without
      its process group leaves the child behind for the test to see.
    - ``commit``: appends a line to each file of ``FAKE_KIRO_COMMIT_FILES`` in the working
      directory and commits them in one commit.
    - ``undeclared``: the same with ``fake-kiro-undeclared.txt``, a file that the tests do
      not declare.
    - ``push``: ``git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/<branch>``, ``<branch>`` being the current
      branch, or ``fake-kiro-push`` on a detached HEAD. The push's exit code is recorded,
      and ``git ls-remote`` against ``remote.origin.url`` (the fetch URL, which
      ``child_env`` does not override) tells whether the ref arrived. A refused push is an
      outcome, not a failure of the behavior.
    - ``push-url``: the same push to the URL in ``remote.origin.url`` instead of ``origin``.
      An override of ``remote.origin.pushurl`` does not stop it.
    - ``switch-main``: in the main working tree of the repository (the first entry of
      ``git worktree list``), creates and switches to the branch ``fake-kiro-switched`` with
      ``git switch -c``, which keeps the uncommitted changes there as they are.
    - ``empty-commit`` (T1): ``git commit --allow-empty`` in the working directory with the
      message ``<token> <address>``, the address being a private-range example
      (:data:`EXAMPLE_ADDRESS`).
    - ``gh-status`` (T3): stands for ``gh auth status`` without running ``gh``, then
      ``git commit --allow-empty`` with the message ``<token> gh-exit=<code>``. The code is
      ``FAKE_KIRO_GH_EXIT`` when set; otherwise 0 when one of the token variables that
      ``gh`` reads first (:data:`GH_TOKEN_VARIABLES`) is set and not empty, and 1 (no
      login) when none is. ``child_env`` drops them, so under layer 3 the code is 1.
    - ``write-file`` (T4): writes ``<token>.txt`` in the working directory only when the
      argv trusts :data:`WRITE_TOOL` (``--trust-tools`` naming it, or ``--trust-all-tools``
      or ``-a``), as a ``kiro-cli`` that enforces the allowlist would. Otherwise it writes
      nothing and records the refusal. A refusal is a result, not a failure.
    - ``write-file-anyway`` (T4): writes ``<token>.txt`` whatever the argv trusts, as a
      ``kiro-cli`` that does not enforce the allowlist would.
    - ``guard-block``: appends to ``HANDOFF_GUARD_LOG`` the entry that the guard wrapper
      writes when a guard stops a tool call, with the token in the call's input, and does
      nothing else. Without ``HANDOFF_GUARD_LOG`` it writes nothing, as a guard whose
      wrapper did not get the variable would. Needs a token in the prompt.
    - ``guard-allow``: the same with the entry of a call that the guard let through.
      Needs a token in the prompt.
    - ``guard-block-other``: the entry of a stopped call whose input does not hold the
      token, as for an unrelated call that a guard stopped.

``FAKE_KIRO_RECORD``
    Absolute path of the record, a JSON Lines file. Unset: nothing is recorded. A relative
    path is a usage error, because it would be resolved in the worktree.
``FAKE_KIRO_ANSWER``
    Written to standard output at the end of a ``chat`` whose behaviors all ran. Default
    ``fake kiro-cli answer`` and a newline; empty writes nothing.
``FAKE_KIRO_STDERR``
    Written to standard error at the end of a ``chat`` whose behaviors all ran, before the
    answer, as the one error line that a real ``kiro-cli`` writes (task 5.9). Empty by
    default, which writes nothing. No newline is added.
``FAKE_KIRO_EXIT_CODE``
    Exit code of such a ``chat``: an integer from 0 to 255, 0 by default.
``FAKE_KIRO_SLEEP_SECONDS``
    Seconds for ``sleep``: a finite number above 0, 30 by default.
``FAKE_KIRO_NOISE_BYTES``
    Bytes per stream for ``noise``: an integer of 0 or more, 1048576 by default.
``FAKE_KIRO_COMMIT_FILES``
    Files for ``commit``, separated by commas: paths relative to the working directory,
    with no empty, ``.``, ``..`` or ``.git`` component. ``fake-kiro-change.txt`` by default.
``FAKE_KIRO_MODE_T0`` to ``FAKE_KIRO_MODE_T4``
    Behaviors for a ``chat`` whose prompt holds a token of that trial, in the form of
    ``FAKE_KIRO_MODE``. Every one that is set is checked on every run, and another name
    that starts with ``FAKE_KIRO_MODE_`` is a usage error, so that a misspelt trial fails.
``FAKE_KIRO_GH_EXIT``
    The code of ``gh-status``: an integer from 0 to 255. Unset by default.
``FAKE_KIRO_VERSION``
    The version that ``--version`` prints after ``kiro-cli``: one or more printable ASCII
    characters without white space. ``0.0.0-fake`` by default, a version that no real
    release has. A test of a selftest that no longer counts changes it.
``HANDOFF_GUARD_LOG``
    Absolute path of the guard log that the guard behaviors append to. Another value is a
    usage error of a ``chat`` that runs a guard behavior. Each entry is one JSON object per
    line, ASCII only, under an exclusive ``flock``: ``schema`` (1), ``at``, ``guard``
    (:data:`FAKE_GUARD`), ``decision`` (``block`` or ``allow``), ``exit_code`` (2 or 0)
    and ``input``, the made-up input of a PreToolUse hook (``hook_event_name``, ``cwd``,
    ``tool_name`` and ``tool_input``).

Record. One JSON object per line, ASCII only, appended under an exclusive ``flock`` so that
runs at the same time do not interleave their lines. A run appends ``start`` (``argv``,
``cwd``, ``pid``, ``ppid``, ``pgid``, ``sid``, the parsed subcommand, options and prompt,
the selftest ``trial`` and ``token`` of the prompt or ``null``, the behaviors and the
variable they came from as ``mode_variable``, and a summary of the environment),
``spawned`` for ``sleep``, and ``end`` (``exit_code``, the result of each behavior,
``error``, and the byte count and SHA-256 of what the run wrote to each stream). A run that
is killed has no ``end``.

The summary of the environment holds every variable name; the values of
``GIT_CONFIG_COUNT``, ``GIT_CONFIG_KEY_<n>`` and ``GIT_CONFIG_VALUE_<n>``, which show the
push URL override (temporary paths in the tests); whether ``GH_CONFIG_DIR``,
``AWS_CONFIG_FILE`` and ``AWS_SHARED_CREDENTIALS_FILE`` name an empty directory or file;
whether ``AWS_EC2_METADATA_DISABLED`` is ``true``; and the SHA-256 of the first entry of ``PATH`` as
``path_first_sha256`` (the entry is a directory under ``HOME`` or the system, so only its hash is
written; a test compares it with the hash of the directory that the harness should put first). No other value is written: not the AWS variables, not tokens, not ``HOME``.

Exit codes: 0 or ``FAKE_KIRO_EXIT_CODE``; 1 for ``whoami-fail`` and ``version-fail``; 2 for a
usage error; 3 when a behavior fails (a git command other than the push fails, or a file
cannot be written); 4 for an unexpected error, with the traceback on standard error.
"""

